WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:03.600
Hello there my name is Carlos Renaso.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.500
I am the writer/director of ¡CALAMBRE!, my
debut feature film about a poet who returns

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.330
to New York City to rekindle an old flame
only to realize that she’s moved on.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:14.360
It’s currently streaming online for as little
as $2.99.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.240
Today I want to share five tips with you for
making a no budget movie.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.240
BUDGET

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.689
We’ll start off with the budget.

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:25.180
Now it varies a bit when you talk to filmmakers
in terms of their process.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.570
Do you consider the budget or the story first?

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.460
Me, I like to go with the budget because you
can build your story around it.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:35.070
Whatever resources are available to you, you
can use it to begin writing.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.070
Now our film ¡CALAMBRE!

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.460
Came a little under $6,000 to get it in the
can.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.810
But we didn’t have the entire $6,000 at
our disposal when we first started shooting.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:44.550
That’s something that really hurt us and
set us back from shooting.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:48.940
We had to pay actors and lockdown locations
but we couldn’t do that since we were shooting

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.560
and paying for everything as we worked our
day jobs and shot check-to-check.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.690
It may seem like the obvious thing to have
all the money up front but not to an eager

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:00.649
filmmaker like me who is just so eager to
finish his first film.

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:01.649
STORY

00:01:01.649 --> 00:01:04.890
You’ve raised the money, now it’s time
to write the story.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.290
What kind of story do you write?

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:09.820
I suggest keeping it simple for comedy or
drama.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.800
For horror or thriller you might need more
money in time for continuity and special effects.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.810
But for comedy or drama your biggest worry
is going to be the dialogue and the performances

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.720
things that as a director are much more manageable
for you.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.860
Our film ¡CALAMBRE! relies heavily on dialogue
and what that allowed me to do was make it

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:29.530
like a hangout movie where people have moments
of disappointment, where they argue, they

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:31.619
laugh or serious moments.

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:34.220
Now one thing you have to be careful of is
the number of characters you have in your

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.220
story.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.520
The more you have, the more of a concern it’s
going to be later when scheduling actors to

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:39.520
shoot.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:40.520
¡CALAMBRE!

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:43.070
Has 6 characters in it and that gave me a
lot of trouble because people work and have

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:44.750
their personal lives as well.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:48.020
So I suggest having three characters at the
most for your story.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:55.090
Great examples of this would be Richard Linklater’s
TAPE and Christopher Nolan’s first moving

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:56.090
FOLLOWING.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.090
LOCATION

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.189
Location, location, it’s all about location.

00:01:58.189 --> 00:02:00.540
As you write your story your visual world
will take place.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:05.049
Ideally you want a location that is easily
accessible to you via your bedroom or your

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:06.049
parent’s house.

00:02:06.049 --> 00:02:08.360
But if you don’t have those things you might
have to pay.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.429
For ¡CALAMBRE! we needed a bar.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:10.880
So we rented out a studio.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.390
But the studio presented us with a big issue
- sound.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:17.810
The studio was located in an industrial area
so there were a lot of trucks outside loading

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:18.810
and unloading.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.790
So it was definitely disruptive.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.290
Then down the hall there was a big wedding
rehearsal going on.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:25.569
You can imagine the nightmare that that was.

00:02:25.569 --> 00:02:27.400
My suggestion is always scout the location
beforehand.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.530
If you’re renting out a place, make sure
to ask if your crew members will be the only

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.280
ones there on shoot day.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:34.280
TRUST

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.940
This is a big one.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.300
Film is a collaborative medium and unless
you’re a jack-of-all-trades and can do everything

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.540
yourself you’re going to need help whether
it’s the sound recordist, cinematographer,

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.060
sound mixer.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.770
Be careful who you trust with your film.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:47.770
I hired a sound mixer.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:51.440
The person took three months to polish up
the sound on ¡CALAMBRE! which is ridiculous

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.460
because ¡CALAMBRE! is only 52 minutes long.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.421
He missed deadlines because of a personal
issue but then it would happen again and again.

00:02:57.421 --> 00:03:01.130
He finally revealed he had taken on another
job just keeping really busy.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.090
How do you avoid this from happening?

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:06.080
Before you hire a sound mixer talk with their
references.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.800
Interview potential candidates if possible.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.610
Give the reasonable strict deadlines and also
tell them not to take on any other jobs if

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:15.909
they’ve taken on your project (especially
when you’ve already paid them).

00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:17.730
FILM FESTIVALS

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.840
You finish your film, now you’re ready to
present it to the world.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.350
What kind of film festivals do you submit
it to?

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.819
Local festivals, international festivals?

00:03:24.819 --> 00:03:27.650
One this is certain you’ve got to have a
plan about your submissions.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.599
I don’t submit to festivals that require
more than $50.00.

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:34.060
And even at $50.00, make sure that festival
caters to your kind of film.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:37.480
You probably shouldn’t submit a comedy in
to a horror film festival.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.890
Secondly, seek out festivals that are in their
first or second year of running.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:44.730
These festivals may be a little bit more receptive
to no budget movies than the bigger, established

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:45.730
film festivals.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:49.840
There are even festivals that do online screenings
which may be beneficial to your film.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.129
Lastly, keep in mind these festivals require
your money to run so be very selective in

00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:56.319
your submission process.

00:03:56.319 --> 00:03:57.319
And there you have it.

00:03:57.319 --> 00:04:01.440
No go make your film and remember, once you’ve
made your film no one can take that away from

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.440
you.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:03.440
My name is Carlos Renaso.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.769
Check out my film ¡CALAMBRE! streaming online.

00:04:05.769 --> 00:04:06.370
Peace!

